+++ b/openspec/changes/archive/2026-11-17-add-create-patch/design.md
@@ -0,0 +1,22 @@
+## Context
+
+`applyPatch` already exists in `src/index.ts` with helpers `clone`, `jsonEqual`, `isObject`, `setMember`. The new function is the inverse operation. RFC 6902 does not define how to compute a diff, only the operations and their semantics (§4); RFC 6901 §3/§4 defines pointer escaping.
+
+## Goals / Non-Goals
+
+**Goals:** patch correct under `applyPatch`; small patches that touch only changed places; no dependencies; no mutation or aliasing.
+**Non-Goals:** the provably shortest patch; `copy`/`test` generation; validating non-JSON input.
+
+## Decisions
+
+- **Recursive diff.** `diff(a, b, path, ops)`: if `jsonEqual`, nothing; if both objects, iterate own keys (`Object.keys`, `Object.hasOwn`) — removals, then recurse on shared keys, then adds; if both arrays, array diff; otherwise `replace` (root path `""` allowed by §4.3). Operation order is chosen so every path is valid when its op is applied.
+- **Array diff via LCS.** Strip common prefix/suffix (by `jsonEqual`), then compute a longest common subsequence of the middle to find unchanged items. Walking the alignment with a running index into the evolving array, each gap between matches with `r` removed and `a` inserted items is handled by recursing into the first `min(r,a)` pairs (an in-place change), then `remove` of the surplus or `add` of the surplus. Items of the old array that are unmatched on one side and appear on the other are therefore expressed as `remove` + `add`; a `move` is not needed because RFC 6902 permits remove+add and the requirement allows either, which keeps the algorithm simple. Surrounding and in-between items are matched by LCS, so are never rewritten. Pairing within a gap could recurse into unrelated items; that is still correct, and still cheaper than or equal to replacing them.
+- **Appends** use the index equal to the array length (not `-`) so paths stay uniform.
+- **Escaping.** `~` → `~0` first, then `/` → `~1` (RFC 6901 §3), matching the existing decoder order.
+- **Safety.** Output objects/arrays for `value` are produced with the existing `clone`, which uses `defineProperty` so `__proto__` stays data. Only own keys are read.
+- **Cost.** LCS is O(n·m) on the trimmed middle; acceptable for payload-sized arrays. Noted as a known limit.
+
+## Risks / Trade-offs
+
+- A reorder of many items yields several remove/add pairs rather than the theoretical minimum; correctness is unaffected.
+- Inputs that are not JSON (`undefined`, functions, cycles) are unsupported; behaviour is unspecified.
